2.2 Flowers and symbols
To make an ikebana you need cut flowers, branches, leaves, seeds or fruits. It is not necessary to use each of them, but if we combine those elements that we choose harmoniously, always preserving asymmetry and respecting the symbols of each element.
In general, every Japanese Ikebana flower arrangement is composed of three groups of flowers in order to represent the Tai-Yo-Fuku triangle (sky-earth-man, respectively). The most important line is the stem that represents Heaven, and which is often called the primary or "Shin."
The tertiary stem or "Hikae," which represents the Earth, is the shortest and is arranged towards the front or slightly to the opposite side of the base of the first two. Once the three main stems are correctly positioned, you can also add additional flowers to complete each arrangement.
Last: open buds, vegetable pods and dry leaves.
The chosen elements are nailed in a 'kenzan' to reproduce landscapes. These, in turn, rest on the container known as 'kaki', filled with water to extend the life of each arrangement.
As soon as creation is complete, take time to contemplate what has been done and reflect on the process of creation. Since as we have advanced, the important thing is not the final action but the path that has led you to that action.
Both curtains and blinds allow us filter outside light and they provide us privacy in our home. Each one does it, however, in a different way, through different installation and opening systems that may be more or less suitable in our home.
With regards to curtain opacity, you will be able to choose from a wide range of possibilities and even combine several to ensure that the atmosphere of the room is pleasant at any time of the year and under any circumstance. Several questions will help you decide between a translucent, an opaque or the combination of both: What uses are you going to give to the room?
Is it a very hot room in summer or very cold in winter?
The curtains lost the battle against the blinds because they represented a more traditional option, however, we have verified that the curtains fit perfectly in modern environments and contemporary, so we would not worry so much about which one fits in which environment as which one we like more or offers us more advantages over the other.
Curtains provide greater control about the amount of light that enters the room by playing with the opening, the fabrics and the layers.
They bring dynamism to the stay, when moving with the step or the wind.
Composed of one piece the blinds are folded vertically, unlike curtains, and allow us to draw a barrier between the outside and the inside of our home. They help to filter the light and prevent the interior of the room from being seen from the outside, protecting us from prying eyes.
They are a highly valued option when it comes to dressing windows in tight spaces.
So, curtains or blinds?
The answer is the curtain. If the question is what is fashionable?
